This is the editorial perspective within one audit, not an external journal decision.

## Is there one stronger publishable story now?

Not yet. There is a coherent technical report about exact artifact contracts and the cost of making persistence claims honest. There is also a useful theory milestone organizing bounded conditioned counts through Fourier data and illustrating a sharp rank-class identifiability threshold. These are related topics, but their juxtaposition does not yet make a theorem-plus-application paper.

The theoretical candidate is mostly standard infrastructure plus a short parity/rectangle corollary. The missing persistence/optimality result remains open. The computational candidate is a reproducible but narrow negative study on one external design with reference backends. Most importantly, the theorem does not predict the performance phenomenon measured. A function may require high-order marginals for identification while admitting a tiny exact representation and cheap queries.

## What has survived

The mathematical proof audit is substantially positive. The rank-threshold formula is correct with its fixed-cut, promised-class assumptions. The profile identity, recompression, quotient bound, and scoped lower bounds also survive. The audit found no need to retract those precisely stated results.

The evidence audit is also positive within scope: all original correctness processes passed, the full timing replay completed, and a new independent finite suite passed. The report's correction of earlier reload accounting is scientifically useful. The correct conclusion is not 'nothing works'; it is 'the stronger claim has not been established'.

## Publication judgment

I would not authorize stronger-paper manuscript synthesis from this merged package now. I would authorize retaining the current technical report with an audit supplement and a conservative contribution statement. A focused expository note or a reproducibility venue may be a reasonable later choice; the present audit does not certify a journal's acceptance threshold.

I do not recommend splitting into two new research papers merely to avoid the integration problem. Neither branch independently supplies the substantial new theorem or externally validated methods contribution requested at this gate. The existing LM and pair-compiler manuscripts must also be protected from overlapping novelty and duplicated negative studies.

## Disagreement retained

Reviewer 6 is more optimistic about immediate value for a narrow report/workshop audience. Reviewer 4 sees expository merit in the rank threshold. I agree with both, while siding with Reviewers 1, 2, and 5 that neither observation warrants a stronger integrated claim. This is a strategic assessment, not a rejection of the correct mathematics.

